2026-07-22T14:42:24.9560903Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9561376Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9561762Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9561956Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9562197Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9562385Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9562606Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9562936Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9563189Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9563394Z             return x + 1
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9563605Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9563769Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9563949Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9564172Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9564397Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9564562Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9564736Z     To execute it::
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9564933Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9565107Z         $ pytest
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9565380Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9565726Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9565942Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9566124Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9566334Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9566562Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9567016Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9567351Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9567535Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9567790Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9568050Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9568295Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9568538Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9568749Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9569100Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9569436Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9569609Z     
2026-07-22T14:42:24.9570568Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
